{"concepts_used":["GC-0072","GC-0013","GC-0036","GC-0081","GC-0116","GC-0084","GC-0014"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMM/Part32A/2/4/2-4-3-b-examples-of-use","quote":"The following endorsement would need to be agreed to: It is a condition of registration that, in use, Rosa varietal names will appear in close proximity to the words DELTA DAWN."},{"ref":"TMM/Part32A/2/1/2-1-2-c-letter-of-consent","quote":"In this circumstance, the owner of the trade mark will need to agree to an endorsement to that effect."},{"ref":"TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-5-b-evidence-of-use","quote":"To achieve this, a proposed trade mark for plants or plant material should be applied to a number of different plants or, if applied to a single plant, always applied in conjunction with the accepted scientific and variety names for that plant."},{"ref":"TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-1-c-trade-names-and-alternate-names~1","quote":"However, both agree that trade marks in use need to be clearly differentiated from the variety name and not used instead of it."}],"answer":"**Who has to follow it?** The Manual describes an endorsement that must be agreed to as part of acceptance. In the separate PBR-consent route, it expressly identifies the trade mark owner as the person who must agree [1–2]. However, the supplied passages do not establish the full legal position on who is bound after registration—for example, subsequent owners or authorised users. I cannot confirm that from these extracts; the supplied Act passages do not resolve it.

**What changes in use? — Manual practice:** For the illustrated varietal-name condition, use of the mark must be accompanied by varietal names in close proximity: the example requires Rosa varietal names to appear close to DELTA DAWN [1]. The Manual also says that, when a plant mark is applied to a single plant, it should always be used with that plant’s accepted scientific and variety names [3]. Its discussion of naming conventions says the trade mark should be clearly differentiated from the variety name, not used instead of it [4].

Thus, the illustrated condition concerns how the mark and varietal information appear together in use [1]. These are statements of **Manual practice**, not a statutory rule establishing everyone legally bound by the condition."}
